{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q2tbeEkvgYKsCMXhBXhG2P","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_7/projects/gen-depth-contamination/literature_map.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"papers:\\\\n  # === Must-Cite ===\\\\n\\\\n  - id: drayson2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Machine-generated text detection prevents language model collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Drayson, Yilmaz, Lampos\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"EMNLP 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.15654\\\\\\\"\\\\n    code: \\\\\\\"https://github.com/GeorgeDrayson/model_collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"verification-curation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"direct-competitor\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Binary MGT detection + importance resampling for collapse prevention; our depth estimator generalizes their binary detector to ordinal depth\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n    methodology: >\\\\n      Binary MGT detector (encoder-only transformer + classification head; best: ModernBERT-150M, AUC .986 in-dist, .943 OOD).\\\\n      Fine-tuned 5 epochs with label smoothing α=0.1 + temperature scaling.\\\\n      Sampling Importance Resampling (SIR): w_j = (1-q(x_j))^b / Σ(1-q(x_l))^b, where q(x) = P(machine-generated), b≥1 bias term.\\\\n      Resample at k=1.5 (50% upsampling). Evaluated on GPT-2 and SmolLM2 (124M–1.7B).\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"SIR filtering not only prevents collapse but improves over human-only training (perplexity -0.85% to -25.65%, diversity +3.44% to +14.62%, MAUVE +8.46% to +20.92%)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"ModernBERT achieves best detection (AUC .986 in-dist); optimal threshold 0.8674 on imbalanced data (29% human)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Curated synthetic data has positive value — outperforms oracle (perfect filter) in equal-ratio settings\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Only evaluates fine-tuning (not pretraining), models ≤2B, open-ended generation only\\\\\\\"\\\\n    positioning_notes: >\\\\n      Closest prior work. Their SIR is a special case of our graduated filtering when K*=1 (binary: human vs all synthetic).\\\\n      We generalize binary→ordinal: estimate depth d∈{0,1,...,K*} and apply depth-graduated weights.\\\\n      K* tells when ordinal signal adds value over binary. They never discuss ordinal depth or multi-class estimation.\\\\n      Our C3 claim directly extends their framework: \\\\\\\"even coarse 3-class signal yields better calibration at decision boundaries.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: schaeffer2025position\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Position: Model Collapse Does Not Mean What You Think\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Schaeffer, Kazdan, Arulandu, Koyejo\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.03150\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"model-collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"framing-critical\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Identifies 8 definitions of model collapse across 3 families; forces us to position K* as discriminability saturation (distribution deformation), not test-loss collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n    methodology: >\\\\n      Position paper. Surveys model collapse literature and identifies 8 distinct definitions organized in 3 families.\\\\n      Evaluates each definition against realistic deployment conditions. No experiments—purely analytical/argumentative.\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"8 definitions in 3 families: (A) Population risk degrading [D1: catastrophic increase, D2: any increase, D3: asymptotic divergence]; (B) Distributions deforming [D4: collapsing variance, D5: scaling law change, D6: mode disappearance/entanglement, D7: tail data loss]; (C) Spurious generation [D8: hallucinated data]\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Under realistic conditions (data accumulation, growing compute), catastrophic risk divergence (D1-D3) is avoidable\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Real harms—tail data loss (D7) and mode entanglement (D6)—have received disproportionately less attention\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Recommends researchers explicitly state which definition they study\\\\\\\"\\\\n    positioning_notes: >\\\\n      K* belongs to Family B (distributions deforming), specifically D4 (collapsing variance) + D6 (mode entanglement).\\\\n      K* measures when distribution deformation renders generations indistinguishable in feature space.\\\\n      K* is NOT about population risk (Family A). This distinction is critical for framing:\\\\n      we contribute a diagnostic for exactly the harms Schaeffer et al. identify as underexplored.\\\\n      Adopt their taxonomy explicitly in Related Work to anchor K* precisely.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: kazdan2025collapse\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Collapse or Thrive? Perils and Promises of Synthetic Data in a Self-Generating World\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Kazdan, Schaeffer, Dey, Gerstgrasser, Rafailov, Donoho, Koyejo\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.16713\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"model-collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"background\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Accumulate vs replace regimes affect contamination dynamics; in accumulate regime K* may shift upward\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n    methodology: >\\\\n      Formal comparison of 3 data regimes: Replace (train on latest synthetic only; Σ→0 a.s.),\\\\n      Accumulate (train on all historical data; real fraction→0 but stability holds),\\\\n      Accumulate-subsample (fixed budget from accumulated pool; intermediate degradation).\\\\n      Theory: Gaussian setting with closed-form limits. Experiments: multivariate Gaussian, KDE, LLM SFT (Gemma2-2B on HelpSteer2/TinyStories).\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Replace: covariance collapses to 0 (a.s.), W2 diverges. Collapse inevitable within 5-10 iterations\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Accumulate: covariance stabilizes at σ₀²·sin(π/√n)/(π/√n). No collapse even as real data fraction→0\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Accumulate-subsample: degradation plateaus rather than diverges\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Cardinality of real data matters more than proportion (R²=0.59 vs 0.34)\\\\\\\"\\\\n    positioning_notes: >\\\\n      Our prompt-chain = replace regime (harshest setting). K*=2 measured under replace conditions.\\\\n      This makes K*=2 a conservative lower bound: under accumulate regime (more realistic), K* would shift upward.\\\\n      Strengthens our result: if depth estimation adds value at K*=2 under replace, it adds more under accumulate.\\\\n      Cite to justify experimental design and contextualize K* as regime-dependent.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: shi2025generalization\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"A Closer Look at Model Collapse: From a Generalization-to-Memorization Perspective\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Shi, Wu, Zhang, Zhang, Tao, Qu\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2025 Spotlight\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16499\\\\\\\"\\\\n    code: \\\\\\\"https://shilianghe007.github.io/model-collapse/\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"model-collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"background\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Generalization-to-memorization transition in iterative retraining parallels our feature-space convergence; entropy decrease over iterations aligns with our depth-discriminative features\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n    methodology: >\\\\n      Study iterative retraining of diffusion models (DDPM) on CIFAR-10 subsets (1K–32K images).\\\\n      Generalization Score (GS): avg distance between generated and nearest training image.\\\\n      Entropy: Kozachenko-Leonenko estimator. Strong correlation: Pearson r=0.91 between entropy and log(GS).\\\\n      Mitigation: entropy-based data selection (greedy max-NN-distance + threshold-decay filter).\\\\n    key_findings:\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"GS drops approximately exponentially across iterations; collapse within 5-10 iterations for large datasets\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Training data entropy is a direct predictor of generalization capability (r=0.91)\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Entropy-based data selection mitigates memorization transition\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Diffusion models only; no LLM experiments. No theoretical analysis of WHY entropy collapses\\\\\\\"\\\\n      - \\\\\\\"Generated images form tight local clusters while overall variance remains relatively stable\\\\\\\"\\\\n    positioning_notes: >\\\\n      Parallel phenomenon across modalities: their entropy decline ↔ our JSD decrease (0.27→0.004).\\\\n      Their \\\\\\\"GS drops exponentially\\\\\\\" ↔ our feature-space divergence vanishes rapidly → K*=2.\\\\n      Key distinction: they measure output-distribution entropy; we measure discriminability in feature space.\\\\n      K* complements their entropy metric: operationalizes \\\\\\\"at what depth does entropy decline = indistinguishability?\\\\\\\"\\\\n      Their clustering-while-variance-stable parallels our depth features converging before global statistics.\\\\n      Cite for cross-modal validation of convergence phenomenon.\\\\n\\\\n  # === Should-Cite ===\\\\n\\\\n  - id: basani2026diveye\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"DivEye: Diversity Boosts AI-Generated Text Detection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Basani, Chen\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"TMLR 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18880\\\\\\\"\\\\n    code: \\\\\\\"https://github.com/IBM/diveye\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"detection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"method-component\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Surprisal diversity features as potential baseline for depth estimation; zero-shot detector outperforms prior work by 33.2%\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: amin2025escaping\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Escaping Collapse: The Strength of Weak Data for Large Language Model Training\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Amin et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.08924\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"verification-curation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"background\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Boosting-inspired framework showing weak signal suffices for iterative training; motivates practical utility of imperfect depth estimators\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: zawalski2026codec\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"CoDeC: Detecting Data Contamination in LLMs via In-Context Learning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Zawalski et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.27055\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"contamination\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Binary contamination detection via ICL confidence shifts; complementary to our finer-grained depth estimation approach\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: gambetta2025surplexity\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Learning by Surprise: Surplexity for Mitigating Model Collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Gambetta et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint 2025\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.12341\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"verification-curation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Surprise-based collapse metric; surplexity scores as potential feature for depth estimation pipeline and baseline comparison\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: zhang2024minkpp\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Min-K%++: Improved Baseline for Detecting Pre-Training Data from Large Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Zhang, Zheng, Gao, Miao\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"EMNLP 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"detection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Binary detection baseline; does not distinguish generational depth\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: shumailov2024collapse\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"AI models collapse when trained on recursively generated data\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Shumailov, Shumaylov, Zhao, Papernot, Anderson, Gal\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"Nature 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"model-collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"background\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Seminal model collapse result; describes macro phenomenon but provides no detection tool for depth\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: alemohammad2024selfconsuming\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Self-Consuming Generative Models Go MAD\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Alemohammad, Casco-Rodriguez, Luber, Babaei, Letizia, Bastani, Baraniuk\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICLR 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"model-collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"background\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Theoretical and empirical analysis of iterative generative training; complements Shumailov with formal convergence analysis\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: dohmatob2024model\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"A Tale of Tails: Model Collapse as a Change of Scaling Laws\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Dohmatob, Feng, Yang, Charton, Kempe\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2024\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"model-collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"background\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Scaling law perspective on collapse; tail distribution loss aligns with our observed feature convergence\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: xie2023dsir\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Data Selection with Importance Resampling\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Xie, Santurkar, Ma, Liang\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"data-selection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Distribution-matching data selection baseline; does not estimate recursive depth\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: tirumala2024d4\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"D4: Improving LLM Pretraining via Document De-Duplication and Diversification\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Tirumala, Markosyan, Zettlemoyer, Aghajanyan\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"data-selection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Deduplication + diversification baseline for data curation; orthogonal to depth-based filtering\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: xie2024doremi\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"DoReMi: Optimizing Data Mixtures Speeds Up Language Model Pretraining\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Xie, Pham, Dong, Du, Liu, Lu, Liang, Le, Ma, Yu\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"data-selection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"evaluation\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Domain reweighting for pretraining; does not consider generational depth dimension\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: kirchenbauer2023watermark\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"A Watermark for Large Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Kirchenbauer, Geiping, Wen, Katz, Miers, Goldstein\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ICML 2023\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"watermarking\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"background\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Provenance tracking via watermarking; complementary approach to depth estimation when watermarks are available\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: zellers2019grover\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Defending Against Neural Fake News\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Zellers, Holtzman, Rashkin, Bisk, Farhadi, Roesner, Choi\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"NeurIPS 2019\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"detection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"background\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Early neural text detection; binary classification paradigm that our ordinal approach generalizes\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  - id: ippolito2020detection\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Automatic Detection of Generated Text is Easiest when Humans are Fooled\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Ippolito, Duckworth, Callison-Burch, Eck\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"ACL 2020\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"detection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"background\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Binary detection difficulty analysis; does not consider multi-generational depth\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n\\\\n  # === New additions (scan 2026-04-28 to 2026-05-14) ===\\\\n\\\\n  - id: falahati2026curated\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Curated Synthetic Data Doesn't Have to Collapse: A Theoretical Study of Generative Retraining with Pluralistic Preferences\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Falahati, Amiri, Larson, Golab\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.07724\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"model-collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"background\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Proves pluralistic reward-based curation prevents collapse via Nash bargaining convergence; directly relevant to our graduated filtering—multi-reward curation is orthogonal to depth-based curation, potentially complementary\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n    date_found: \\\\\\\"2026-05-14\\\\\\\"\\\\n    positioning_notes: >\\\\n      Shows that curation strategy matters for collapse prevention: single-reward collapses, multi-reward stabilizes.\\\\n      Our depth estimator provides a different curation axis: instead of multiple rewards, we use generational depth.\\\\n      Potential synergy: combine their pluralistic rewards with our depth signal for even stronger filtering.\\\\n      Does NOT address generational depth or K*. No direct challenge to K*=2.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: mady2026feataugmented\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Feature-Augmented Transformers for Robust AI-Text Detection Across Domains and Generators\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Mady, Reschke, Schuller\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.03969\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"detection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"method-component\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Attention-based linguistic feature fusion for cross-domain/cross-generator binary detection; DeBERTa-v3-base+FeatAttn achieves 85.9% balanced accuracy under distribution shift on M4. Feature augmentation architecture applicable to depth estimation pipeline\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n    date_found: \\\\\\\"2026-05-14\\\\\\\"\\\\n    positioning_notes: >\\\\n      Binary detector (human vs AI) with strong cross-generator generalization via feature augmentation.\\\\n      Their FeatAttn module could be adapted for our ordinal depth classifier—fuse linguistic features with encoder representations.\\\\n      Key limitation: binary only, no depth awareness. In-domain 99.5% but brittle under shift.\\\\n      Our K* result implies even perfect binary detection hits diminishing returns past depth 2.\\\\n\\\\n  - id: pu2026generator\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Breaking the Generator Barrier: Disentangled Representation for Generalizable AI-Text Detection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Pu, Cheng, Yuan, Wu, Bi\\\\\\\"\\\\n    venue: \\\\\\\"arXiv preprint 2026\\\\\\\"\\\\n    url: \\\\\\\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.13692\\\\\\\"\\\\n    category: \\\\\\\"detection\\\\\\\"\\\\n    role: \\\\\\\"method-component\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Generator-agnostic detection via disentangled representations (dual-bottleneck + cross-view regularization); up to 24.2% accuracy gain on MAGE benchmark (20 LLMs). Disentanglement of semantic vs generator artifacts is architecturally relevant for our depth estimator\\\\\\\"\\\\n    cited: false\\\\n    date_found: \\\\\\\"2026-05-14\\\\\\\"\\\\n    positioning_notes: >\\\\n      Key insight: separate semantic content from generator-specific artifacts parallels our need to isolate depth signal from generator identity.\\\\n      Dual-bottleneck architecture enforces compact, minimal latent codes; could inspire our depth encoder design.\\\\n      Tests on 20 LLMs across 7 categories but only binary classification. No multi-generation or depth estimation.\\\\n      Our depth estimator needs similar generator invariance: depth signal should hold across model families.\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 16560, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01WwG7Aq1RUmx67aCcPtNmEQ","session_id":"d37fb44e-7e5b-48a2-b4c1-fa5a7ae0cff1","uuid":"624955a7-1bdd-4043-89ea-bf7bae9105a2","timestamp":"2026-05-18T12:09:42.022Z"}
